<h2>The closest, largest onion relationship India has</h2>
<p>Bangladesh is consistently India's largest onion destination by value — the trade data we've verified reports approximately USD 187 million, well ahead of the next-largest markets. That scale reflects pure proximity economics: short transit and minimal freight cost make Indian onion structurally the cheapest reliable option for Bangladeshi buyers, in a way that no competing origin can easily match.</p>

<h2>The policy caveat you need to understand before ordering</h2>
<p>We'd rather explain this clearly than have you discover it mid-negotiation. India actively manages onion exports to protect domestic supply and consumer prices — using a minimum export price, at times an export duty, and, in 2023-24, an outright six-month export ban. These measures have changed with limited notice more than once.</p>
<p>Because of that, we don't quote onion business as though policy conditions were fixed and settled. When you place an order, we confirm the applicable terms at that point — MEP, any duty, licensing requirements — and your contract reflects what's actually in force. If you're building a recurring Bangladesh onion programme, we'd encourage planning for that variability explicitly rather than assuming a stable baseline.</p>

<h2>Sea or land: a real routing decision</h2>
<p>Bangladesh is one of the few markets where the sea-vs-land choice genuinely matters:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sea to Chattogram</strong> (2-5 days) — suits full container loads and onion sourced from anywhere in India's growing regions.</li>
  <li><strong>Land via Petrapole-Benapole</strong> (1-3 days) — often faster and cheaper when the onion is sourced in western India, but throughput varies with border congestion and has its own documentation pattern separate from sea freight.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tell us your sourcing preference and volume, and we'll recommend a routing rather than defaulting to one.</p>

<h2>Specification and packing</h2>
<p>Specify a size grade explicitly — 40-60mm, 50-70mm or 60-80mm — since preferences differ by buyer segment even within Bangladesh. Packing is mesh or net bags (5-25kg) for airflow; properly cured onion in ventilated packaging is what survives the trip in good condition, and sealed formats are not something we'd recommend regardless of what packaging a buyer's retail programme might otherwise prefer.</p>

<h2>Documentation</h2>
<p>Phytosanitary certificate, certificate of origin (SAFTA certificate where preferential tariff treatment applies), and documentation reflecting whatever export-policy conditions are in force at the time of shipment. See our <a href="/markets/bangladesh/">Bangladesh market guide</a> for the full documentation and payment-norms picture.</p>
